## Build First, Monetize Later

We will launch with user value first (trusted listings, fast search, verification) and keep monetization paths open behind the scenes.

### MVP Scope (Phase 1)
- [Implemented] Public property browsing with county, price, and amenities filters.
- [Implemented] Agent and landlord onboarding with admin verification workflow.
- [Partial] **Authentication**: Email/Password signup + **Google Social Login** for fast onboarding.
- [Planned] Property management dashboard for verified agents.
- [Partial] WhatsApp contact flow for fast inquiry conversion.
- [Implemented] Fraud reporting and listing moderation queue.
- [Implemented] **Recommended for You** feed using location, design style, and interior preferences.
- [Implemented] **FindAHome Chatbot** for guided search, FAQs, and support routing.

### Architecture Decisions That Keep Monetization Open
- [Implemented] **Multi-role accounts now**: `house_hunter`, `agent`, `landlord`, `admin`.
- [Planned] **Organization model now**: agents/landlords can later upgrade to paid tiers without changing identity tables.
- [Planned] **Feature flags now**: gate premium capabilities without rewiring frontend routes.
- [Partial] **Event logging now**: store actions (`listing_created`, `lead_sent`, `listing_boost_click`) to support future billing and analytics.
- [Planned] **Soft limits now**: enforce free-tier caps via config so paid limits can be activated later.

### Data Model Additions To Include From Day 1 (Not Yet Charged)
- [Planned] `organizations` (owner, name, verification status)
- [Planned] `organization_members` (roles and permissions)
- [Planned] `plans` (free/pro/business definitions)
- [Planned] `organization_plan_state` (active plan, quotas, renewal metadata)
- [Planned] `usage_events` (meterable actions per organization)

### Personalization Data Model (For Recommendations)
- [Implemented] `user_preference_profile` (preferred counties, budget bands, bedrooms, furnishing, style tags)
- [Implemented] `listing_style_tags` (design and interior tags such as `modern`, `minimal`, `open_kitchen`, `scandinavian`)
- [Implemented] `listing_view_events` (user, listing, county, viewed_at, dwell_time)
- [Implemented] `listing_save_events` (saved/shortlisted listings)
- [Implemented] `recommendation_impressions` (what was recommended, rank, clicked/not clicked)

These tables can stay on `free` for all users until monetization is enabled.

## Recommended for You (Kenya-Focused)

### Goal
Show each user homes they are most likely to like based on:
- **Location intent**: county and nearby areas they repeatedly browse.
- **Most visited house designs**: bungalow, maisonette, apartment, studio, bedsitter, etc.
- **Interior preferences**: style tags and features like open kitchen, tiled floors, modern finish, furnished.

### Data Inputs
- `user_preference_profile`: preferred counties, budget range, bedroom count, furnishing, and style tags.
- `listing_view_events`: used to learn repeated county interest and dwell time signals.
- `listing_save_events`: used to boost houses the user shortlists.
- `listing_style_tags`: used to measure design and interior affinity.
- `recommendation_impressions`: used to log what was shown and whether the user clicked it.

### Ranking Model
We score each verified listing with a simple weighted formula:

$$
Score = 0.45(LocationMatch) + 0.30(DesignAffinity) + 0.20(InteriorAffinity) + 0.05(Freshness)
$$

- `LocationMatch`: same county or nearby county boost.
- `DesignAffinity`: overlap between style tags and house-design behavior (`property_type`) from views/saves.
- `InteriorAffinity`: budget, bedrooms, and furnishing fit.
- `Freshness`: recently verified listings rank slightly higher.

Status: [Implemented] in backend endpoint `GET /api/recommended-listings/`.

### API Flow
- `GET /api/recommended-listings/` returns ranked listings plus impression IDs for click tracking.
- `POST /api/user-preferences/` and `PATCH /api/user-preferences/{id}/` save the preference profile.
- `POST /api/listing-view-events/` records listing views.
- `POST /api/listing-save-events/` records shortlist actions.
- `PATCH /api/recommendation-impressions/{id}/` marks a recommended card as clicked.

### Frontend Flow
- The Recommendations page loads the ranked feed from the backend API.
- The Search page records views and shortlist actions from listing cards.
- The Preferences page lets users set counties, budget bands, bedrooms, furnishing, and style tags.

### Cold Start Rules
- New users see verified, trending homes in their selected or most active counties.
- If preference history is sparse, county and freshness signals carry more weight.
- If there is no saved preference profile yet, the UI encourages the user to complete it.

### Budget-Friendly Implementation
- Current MVP: request-time weighted scoring using preference profile + behavioral events.
- Planned optimization: nightly batch recommendations and materialized affinity views.
- Planned optimization: cache top recommendations per user for fast mobile loading.
- Current fallback: if user history is sparse, county and freshness signals dominate.

### Success Metrics
- Recommendation click-through rate (CTR).
- Save/shortlist rate from recommended cards.
- Inquiry conversion rate from recommendations.
- Time-to-first-inquiry after signup.

## Scalability Baseline (Budget Friendly)
- Keep backend stateless so horizontal scaling is easy.
- Use PostgreSQL indexes for `county`, `price`, `status`, and `verified`.
- Store images in object storage/CDN, not in app server disk.
- Add pagination and cached filter results for high-traffic counties.
- Use background workers only for heavy jobs (image optimization, alerts).

## Kenya-First Product Guardrails
- Mobile-first UI and low-data image strategy.
- County-centric search and filtering across all 47 counties.
- English and Kiswahili content support.
- Verification-first trust model for agents and listings.
- Keep payments optional in MVP; integrate M-Pesa when monetization starts.

## Roles, Permissions, and UI Access

Use **RBAC** (Role-Based Access Control) with object-level checks.

### Roles
- `admin`: full system visibility and control.
- `landlord`: manages and views only data tied to own listings.
- `agent`: manages and views only data tied to own listings (or assigned organization listings).
- `house_hunter`: browse/search/save/inquire only; no listing management access.

### Permission Boundaries
- `admin`
  - Can view and manage all users, listings, reports, verification queues, and analytics.
- `landlord`
  - Can create/edit/archive own listings.
  - Can view inquiries, saves, performance stats, and reports for own listings only.
  - Cannot access other landlords'/agents' listing data.
- `agent`
  - Same scope as landlord, but for assigned listings/organization.
  - Cannot access listings outside assignment scope.
- `house_hunter`
  - Can view public verified listings, recommended feed, and own profile/saved items/inquiries.
  - Cannot access management dashboards, moderation, or internal analytics.

### Do Agent and Landlord Need Separate UI?
Not necessarily separate apps. Use **one app with role-specific dashboards**:
- Shared shell/navigation.
- Different menu items and pages by role.
- Reuse listing management components for both agent and landlord.

This is cheaper, faster, and easier to maintain than building separate UIs.

### Recommended UI Structure
- `/admin/*` for admin tools (moderation, verification, user management).
- `/dashboard/*` for landlord and agent tools.
- `/app/*` (or public routes) for house hunters.

The route guard decides access by role and ownership.

### Backend Enforcement (Critical)
Hide/show UI is not security. Enforce permissions on API too:
- Role checks at endpoint level.
- Object-level ownership checks at query level.
- Return `403` for unauthorized access attempts.

### Ownership Rules (Examples)
- Listing update allowed only if:
  - user is `admin`, or
  - user is `landlord` and `listing.owner_id == user.id`, or
  - user is `agent` and listing belongs to user organization/assignment.

### Audit and Safety
- Log sensitive actions (`listing_updated`, `status_changed`, `verification_approved`).
- Soft delete important entities where needed.
- Keep immutable verification history for trust and dispute resolution.

## Chatbot Plan (Build Now, Expand Later)

### What the Chatbot Should Do in MVP
- Help house hunters find listings faster using natural language:
  - Example: "2-bedroom in Nairobi under 35k with borehole and fiber"
- Answer common questions (verification process, how to contact agent, reporting fake listings).
- Guide users to key actions (save listing, contact on WhatsApp, set alert).
- Route account/support issues to human support when needed.

### Role-Aware Chatbot Behavior
- `house_hunter`: search assistant, listing guidance, saved-items help, trust/safety help.
- `agent` and `landlord`: listing management help, verification status help, dashboard how-to.
- `admin`: moderation workflow helper and quick access to admin actions.

The chatbot should respect the same RBAC rules as the UI and API. It must never reveal private data outside role scope.

### Budget-Friendly Architecture
- [Implemented] Phase 1: retrieval + rules-based assistant (lowest cost).
  - FAQ knowledge base from product policies/help articles.
  - Structured search query parser for listing intents (county, budget, bedrooms, features).
- [Planned] Phase 2: add LLM for richer conversation once usage grows.
- [Planned] Cache repeated FAQ responses and top intent results.
- [Partial] Add strict token and request limits per user/session.

### Chatbot Data and Logging
- [Implemented] `chat_sessions` (user, role, started_at, channel)
- [Implemented] `chat_messages` (session, sender, intent, text, response_time_ms)
- [Implemented] `chat_intent_events` (parsed intent fields: county, budget, bedrooms, amenities)
- [Partial] `chat_handoff_events` (escalated_to_human, reason)

### Safety and Quality Controls
- Do not provide legal/financial guarantees.
- Always mark uncertain answers and suggest verified listing details.
- Add abuse filters and rate limits.
- Log unresolved intents to improve the knowledge base weekly.

### Kenya-Focused Chatbot Intents
- Rent and location queries by county/sub-location.
- Utility-focused filters (borehole, fiber provider, backup power).
- Commute-focused guidance (distance to stage/main road).
- Language support: English first, Kiswahili follow-up.

## 6-Week Build Plan
### Week 1
- Setup Django project, custom user model, auth, and roles.
- Implement Google OAuth (social login) and JWT token flow.
- Setup Next.js app shell and shared UI primitives.

### Week 2
- Implement listing CRUD, media upload, and property attributes.
- Build admin verification flows and moderation statuses.

### Week 3
- Build search/filter API and frontend results pages.
- Add county and amenity filters (water, fiber, backup power).
- Capture view/save events needed for recommendation signals.

### Week 4
- Add agent dashboard, listing performance basics, and WhatsApp inquiry actions.
- Add report listing flow and moderation queue.
- Build Recommended for You endpoint with location + design/interior scoring.
- Implement chatbot MVP (intent parser + FAQ + search handoff).

### Week 5
- Improve performance (caching, indexes, pagination).
- Add audit logs and usage events for future monetization.
- Add recommendation caching and ranking quality checks.
- Add chatbot logging, analytics, and unresolved-intent review loop.

### Week 6
- QA, seed realistic Kenya data, and launch prep.
- Track KPIs: verified listings, inquiry rate, report resolution time.

## Monetization Activation Later (Phase 2)
When ready, enable without major rewrites:
- Featured listings (boost visibility)
- Agent subscriptions (higher limits and analytics)
- Pay-per-lead bundles
- Partner referrals (moving, internet, insurance)

Because plans, usage events, and organization state already exist, this becomes a feature rollout instead of a platform rewrite.

